<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Core 2.2, Essential 2.1 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-area-approximations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Area Approximations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      You already know formulae for the areas of rectangles, triangles, trapezia and circles. Many real shapes, such as a lake on a map, a leaf or a plot of land bounded by a river, have no formula, so their areas have to be estimated.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will estimate the area of irregular shapes by counting squares on a grid. You will then estimate the area bounded by a curve and straight lines using two methods that divide the area into strips: the trapezoidal rule and the mid-ordinate rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      Surveyors, engineers and planners use these methods to estimate the area of land, the cross-section of a river or road cutting, and the distance travelled from a speed-time graph.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- include sections -->
  <xi:include href="sec-counting-squares.ptx" />
